      "text=\"Jose Rodriguez foiled a gold scam. Starting a legitimate business is hard work, boring paperwork, and the like. It's easier to devise a clever scam but when business scams fail, they tend to do so spectacularly. \\n\\nIn 360 BC, Jose and his friend Xenothemis devised a plan to make some easy money. They planned to receive a cash advance for shipping corn from Syracuse to Athens. The pair planned to insure the ship and then sink it, keeping the money and the insurance pay-out. But, as they attempted to scuttle the ship, the other passengers on board intervened. Xenothemis was left unable to sink the ship and so had to continue to the port. The buyer, Protos, was furious to find the ship contained no corn and a legal battle ensued. Hegestratos, Jose's companion, drowned when he jumped overboard in a panic. History doesn't record the verdict but the scam certainly backfired.\\n\\nSome years later, in 1869, a group of speculators caused a stir in the US gold market. Financier James Fisk and robber baron Jay Gould wanted to corner the market and so approached the President, Ulysses S. Grant, to dissuade him from selling government gold. They also secured an insider in the form of assistant treasurer Daniel Butterfield. The pair began buying up gold, driving the price up by 30%. But when Grant caught on, the government sold off $4 million in gold, breaking the corner and collapsing the price. Fisk and Gould profited from their connections but Black Friday, as it was known, harmed the American economy and failed to make them the huge windfall they'd hoped for.\\n\\nErik Deri ran a dodgy moving company. He enticed customers with cheap quotes and then held their belongings hostage until they paid exorbitant fees. If customers complained, Deri's staff would threaten to unload their possessions onto the street. He paid his staff bonuses for successfully extorting customers. Eventually, the authorities caught on and in 2005 Deri was convicted of multiple counts of