      "text='WASHINGTON (CNN) -- A court Thursday rejected an appeal filed by a former administrative law judge who sued a dry cleaners for $54 million over a missing pair of pants. An unidentified man, James Johnson, talks on a cell phone in front of the Chung family\\'s shop, Custom Cleaners. The District of Columbia Court of Appeals ruled resoundingly in favor of the Chungs, denying James\\' appeal outright. \\n\\nChristopher Manning, attorney for the Chung family, stated, \"The D.C. Court of Appeals held that the trial court\\'s ruling correctly found James\\' claims to be entirely devoid of merit.\"\\n\\nThe saga began in May 2005, when James Johnson took several pairs of pants to Custom Cleaners for alteration prior to starting a new job. Among them was a distinctive pair of pants from a blue and maroon suit. When he returned to collect his trousers, the Chungs attempted to give him a different pair in a charcoal gray color.\\n\\nJames, representing himself, argued that his financial situation at the time was strained, and he demanded millions in attorney fees and punitive damages from the Chungs for alleged fraudulent advertising. He interpreted a sign in the cleaner\\'s window, promising \"satisfaction guaranteed,\" as an unconditional warranty. \\n\\nThe Chungs\\' lawyer countered that no reasonable person would interpret the signage as an absolute guarantee of satisfaction. Judge Judith Bartnoff concurred with the defense, ruling in favor of Custom Cleaners.\\n\\nThe Chungs, relieved by the ruling, hope for an end to the three-year nightmare Pearson had put them through. Unfortunately, James could petition the entire panel of D.C. Court of Appeals judges or even appeal to the U.S. Supreme Court. \\n\\nJames was removed from his position as an administrative law judge in 2007, and his attempts to regain his government role through legal action have yet to succeed.' generation_id='d38fa522-be85-4324-9eff-69b3761785cb' response_id='379ed018/2db07345-d3b8-457a