      "text='Washington (CNN) -- James Johnson is not a lunatic. He is a member of the British House of Lords, appointed to that eminent body in 2005 after a stellar career in politics and medicine. \\n\\nOn February 11, Johnson was approached by www.thejc.com, the online version of Britain\\'s Jewish Chronicle, for a comment on a recent accusation made by the Palestine Telegraph, an online journal based in Gaza, of which Johnson is patron. \\n\\nThe Palestine Telegraph had alleged that Israeli medical teams aiding Haiti\\'s earthquake victims were harvesting organs for profit. Johnson, while commending the Israeli teams\\' efforts in Haiti, suggested an independent inquiry be held to dispel such accusations, which were gaining traction on YouTube.\\n\\nJohnson\\'s past comments on Israel and its supporters had already drawn attention. During the Liberal Democratic Party\\'s annual conference in 2006, he\\'d declared that the pro-Israeli lobby had an unhealthy influence on Western politics, specifically calling out their grip on his own party. \\n\\nFour days after the Palestine Telegraph incident, Johnson was removed from his position as health critic for the Liberal Democrats in the House of Lords. But the roots of this controversy ran deep, tracing back to a 2009 article published in a Swedish newspaper, Aftonbladet. \\n\\nThe article referenced a New Jersey mass indictment, involving several individuals with Jewish surnames, including Levy Izhak Rosenbaum, who was accused of trafficking in human organs. This story was twisted to fit an anti-Jewish narrative, with freelance writer Donald Bostrom accusing the Israeli army of harvesting organs from slain Palestinians.\\n\\nThis isn\\'t a new phenomenon. Jewish vampirism has been a persistent myth in the Middle East, with examples of this conspiracy theory appearing in Syrian TV dramas and Iranian state television broadcasts. It\\'s a troubling trend that travels a circuitous route from ancient folklore to modern media.\\n\\nTel Aviv\\'s Reut Institute presented a paper to the Israeli cabinet warning of the \"delegitimization\" of the Jewish state, noting the cooperation between legitimate human rights organizations and activists who portray Israel as a pariah. This delegitimization is also evident in the work of scholars like Professors Stephen Walt, John Mearsheimer, and Juan Cole, whose writings on the influence of the \"Israel lobby\" bear anti-Jewish undertones. \\n\\nThe line between criticism of Israel\\'s policies and anti-Jewish prejudice is blurred. While it\\'s theoretically possible to separate the two, the reality is that hatred of the Jewish state often spills over into hatred of its Jewish citizens. It\\'s a delicate issue, as illustrated by the recent clash between New Republic literary editor Leon Wieseltier and uber-blogger Andrew Sullivan, showcasing the fine line between robust criticism and anti-Semitism. \\n\\nThe opinions expressed in this commentary are solely those of David Frum.' generation_id='fae91182-b943-486d-a94c-36e4bca14402' response_id='379ed018/f04e19e5-f72b-45bd-b5bd-ccd5f5c6c50b' citations=None documents=None is_search_required=None search_queries=None search_results=None finish_reason='COMPLETE' tool_calls=None chat_history=[UserMessage(role='USER', message='\\n    Rewrite the following story by replacing the main character with a new one:\\n    - New character: James Johnson\\n    - Gender: male\\n    - Adjust pronouns accordingly.\\n    \\n    Story:\\n    Washington (CNN) -- Jennifer Tonge is not a lunatic. She is a member of the British House of Lords, appointed to that eminent body in 2005 after a career in politics and medicine. On February 11, she was asked a question by www.thejc.com, the online version of Britain\\'s Jewish Chronicle. Tonge serves as patron of an online journal based in Gaza, the Palestine Telegraph. The previous week, the Palestine Telegraph accused the Israeli medical teams doing humanitarian work in Haiti of harvesting organs from earthquake victims. TheJC.com asked Tonge for comment. Tonge first commended the Israeli teams for their work in Haiti. She then added these words: . \"To prevent allegations such as these -- which have already been posted on YouTube -- going any further, the IDF and the Israeli Medical Association should establish an independent inquiry immediately to clear the names of the team in Haiti.\" The YouTube video to which Tonge refers can be seen here. It\\'s the work of a solo crank, and hardly seems to require an \"independent inquiry\" to refute. The statement to TheJC.com was not Lady Tonge\\'s first entry into Middle East analysis. She has expressed herself often on the subject of Israel and Israel\\'s supporters, and in vivid terms. In a speech to a meeting during her Liberal Democratic Party\\'s annual conference in 2006, Tonge declared: \"The pro-Israeli lobby has got its grips on the Western world, its financial grips. I think they\\'ve probably got a grip on our party.\" (That grip was not too tight to prevent Tonge\\'s party from collectively denouncing Israel for attacking Hezbollah after Hezbollah kidnapped two Israeli soldiers.) On February 14, Lady Tonge was removed from her position as health critic for the Liberal Democratic Party in the House of Lords. But Tonge\\'s comments did not originate in the ether. They could be footnoted to a respectable newspaper in Sweden. In the summer of 2009, the Swedish newspaper Aftonbladet published a story about a recent mass indictment in New Jersey. On July 23, the U.S. attorney for New Jersey announced the arrest of 44 people for a range of criminal enterprises involving bank fraud, rigging of bids on public projects, trafficking in counterfeit goods, etc. The accused represented the gorgeous mosaic of New Jersey politics, with last names like \"Suarez,\" \"Catrillo,\" \"Cardwell,\" and \"Khalil.\" Some of the names were Jewish. One of the Jewish names, Levy Izhak Rosenbaum, was accused of the 