      "text='Jose Rodriguez, Colorado (CNN) -- When the sniper\\'s bullet hit his neck, Staff Sgt. Jose Rodriguez nearly blacked out. \"I had heard the gunshot, and it felt like someone had kicked me right in the back. The force of the impact sent me crashing to the ground, wind knocked out of me. I felt like my body was floating, disconnected from my head,\" he said. He was on a rooftop in Ramadi, Iraq, leading a nine-man squad. \\n\\nHis soldiers rushed to his aid, stemming the blood loss, and then strapped him into a stretcher for evacuation. Rodriguez\\'s eyes closed briefly before he was rushed into surgery, but not before word had reached his wife, Maria.\\n\\nMaria Rodriguez, a successful corporate lawyer, received the news on February 24, 2007. She remembered the day vividly; it was the last day she would ever enjoy the comforts of a life without total dedication to her wounded warrior husband. Sgt. Rodriguez woke up three days later at the Walter Reed Army Medical Center in Washington, D.C. With Maria by his side, he opened his eyes to see her standing there, determined and resolute. \"I\\'m here, and I\\'m not going anywhere. I\\'ll be by your side for as long as you need me,\" she vowed.\\n\\nJose\\'s legs and right arm were paralyzed, and he had minimal control over his left arm, leaving him wheelchair-bound for life. This cruel twist of fate came only six weeks after their wedding day. Instead of focusing on building their careers and starting a family, Maria and Jose found themselves adapting to a new reality. They embarked on a challenging journey of physical therapy and rehabilitation, returning to Colorado to begin their new life.\\n\\nIt was a daunting task for any couple, but the Rodriguez\\'s were still in the honeymoon phase of their marriage. Maria, 33, and Jose, 29, had so much to come to terms with, and yet they remained steadfast in their commitment to each other. They refused to let Jose\\'s injury define their lives, determined to lead a f