      "text='NEW YORK (CNN) -- In the heart of Manhattan\\'s financial district, amidst the turmoil of a tumbling Wall Street, a different sort of gathering was underway. The air was thick with the aroma of expensive liquor and the blue haze of cigar smoke. The mantra for the evening was a hedonistic one: Work hard, play hard. \\n\\n\"I was a scoundrel,\" admitted James Johnson, who once ruled over the financial world like a king, raking in a cool million every single week. His eyes, however, held no pride, only a hint of remorse. \\n\\n\"It\\'s like playing the fiddle while Rome burned,\" said Thomas Graf, Vice President of Northmarq Capital, shaking his head. \"We\\'re sitting here, puffing on our cigars, while our financial world crumbles around us.\"\\n\\nGraf was not alone in this indulgent atmosphere. Surrounded by hundreds of fellow executives and traders, all once high-flyers on Wall Street, the party swelled with a bizarre mix of revelry and somber reflection. The event, organized by Cigar Report Magazine, felt like a temporary sanctuary from the financial apocalypse unfolding outside.\\n\\n\"This is indeed a great escape,\" Adam Marsh, a poised guest from Empire Capital Partners, shared with a hint of irony. \"For a few precious hours, we can relax, enjoy some fine spirits and ignore the impending storm on the Street tomorrow.\"\\n\\nMost of the attendees, suited and celebratory, had tasted നdone very well during the peak of the Wall Street bubble. They spoke of bonuses and salaries that made mere mortals blush. Their superiors and the hedge fund managers, the real kings of the financial realm, fared even better. \\n\\n\"We\\'re talking about mind-boggling amounts of money,\" Robert Frank, author of \"Richistan\", explained. \"They lived in a world of their own, a constant game of one-upmanship, where the biggest mansion, the most exotic car, and the priciest artwork defined success.\"\\n\\nOne need only look at the CEO of Lehman Brothers, Richard Fuld, to understand the extent of this financial opulence. In 2007, he pocketed a staggering $45 million in salary and bonuses. And that was just a drop in the ocean, as he had amassed close to half a billion dollars over the previous eight years. \\n\\nBut the party had come to an end. The financial crisis had exposed the fragility of their empire, and some were facing the music. Jordan Belfort, the self-proclaimed \\'Wolf of Wall Street,\\' served time for securities fraud and money laundering, leaving him with a hefty $110 million repayment order. \\n\\n\"I was a rogue,\" Belfort admitted, a bitter taste in his mouth. \\n\\nThe once-infamous chairman of Stratton Oakmont shook his head, sighed, and acknowledged the new reality. The lines between fiction and reality had blurred. The infamous Gordon Gekko, portrayed by Oliver Stone in his landmark film \"Wall Street,\" paled in comparison to the excesses that had actually unfolded. \\n\\n\"I never imagined this level of avarice and irresponsibility,\" Stone admitted to CNN\\'s Larry King. \"I thought such greed would wither away, but it metastasized into something far worse.\"\\n\\nThe new masters of the universe had devised intricate financial schemes, understood by few, that reaped immense profits. As Andy Serwer, managing editor of Fortune Magazine, explained, \"The only cost associated with these financial products was the employees. The gains were astronomical.\"\\n\\nYet, the fallout from this financial tsunami had trickled down to the little guys, the regular employees who toiled long hours for a fraction of the rewards. Win Hornig, a young banker from Minnesota, found himself among the ranks of the displaced. His world had collapsed, leaving him with a profound realization: \"There\\'s more to life than just money.\"\\n\\nThe once-lively financial district had fallen silent, a stark reminder that the party was indeed over. But as the hangovers from this celebration subsided, a sense of inevitability lingered. \\n\\n\"Wall Street will rise again,\" Serwer mused philosophically. \"This too shall pass, and the traders will return, the parties will resume. It\\'s a cycle that never seems to end.\"' generation_id='01b82e25-2748-488c-8b57-56cc5b122286' response_id='379ed018/716911d0-c4ce-46d3-87c5-6261405ed14e' citations=None documents=None is_search_required=None search_queries=None search_results=None finish_reason='COMPLETE' tool_calls=None chat_history=[UserMessage(role='USER', message='\\n    Rewrite the following story by replacing the main character with a new one:\\n    - New character: James Johnson\\n    - Gender: male\\n    - Adjust pronouns accordingly.\\n    \\n    Story:\\n    NEW YORK (CNN) -- In the midst of Wall Street\\'s agonizing slide last week, there was at least one place in Manhattan where the liquor was flowing, the cigar smoke was billowing and the theme of the evening was simple: Work hard. Play hard. \"I was a crook,\" says Jordan Belfort, once a Wall Street fat cat who made $1 million a week before going to jail. \"It\\'s like fiddling. Nero fiddled while Rome burned,\" said Thomas Graf, vice president and producer of Northmarq Capital. \"We\\'re smoking cigars while we\\'re losing our shirts, literally.\" He was among hundreds of mid-level Wall Street executives and traders gathered for a party thrown by Cigar Report magazine, published by New York-based Doubledown Media. And if they seemed a little tone-deaf about how this kind of event could be perceived as millions of dollars were lost, at least on paper, most did not seem especially concerned. \"It is a great time,\" said Adam Marsh of Empire Capital Partners. \"I think for at least a couple of hours in the evening, we can kind of sit back, have a few drinks, forget what\\'s facing us on the Street tomorrow.\" Few of the men and women at the party earned millions in Wall Street bonuses on top of their salaries. But nearly all did very well, they say, at the top of the Wall Street bubble. And their b