ed Monti to remain in his caretaker role until a new government is formed, emphasizing the importance of Monti\\'s leadership for Italy\\'s economy and relations with Europe. The current government\\'s mandate requires Napolitano\\'s intervention meetings this week to either confirm Bersani as prime minister or result in a technocratic leader, with early elections inevitable, likely in June or the following autumn. \\n\\nAdding further complexity, Napolitano\\'s presidential term ends on May 15. Italian law forbids the president from dissolving parliament or calling new elections within the final six months of their tenure. Therefore, parliament must begin the process of electing a new president to succeed Napolitano around April 15. \\n\\nPotential candidates for the presidency, discussed by members of parliament, include Gianni Letta, Berlusconi\\'s center-right ally, and Emma Bonino, a highly regarded member of the Radical Party and former EU parliamentarian. Berlusconi has signalled he might support Bersani\\'s appointment as prime minister if Letta were to become president. Other names also circulate in the Italian press.\\n\\nThe upcoming weeks will determine Italy\\'s political destiny, with the possibility of a new presidential election and a fresh mandate to break the current stalemate.' generation_id='36ac6858-1517-4226-a53e-540cc0b8a7d1' response_id='379ed018/8dbc7232-1a90-48e7-823d-0161ff3d30c1' citations=None documents=None is_search_required=None search_queries=None search_results=None finish_reason='COMPLETE' tool_calls=None chat_history=[UserMessage(role='USER', message='\\n    Rewrite the following story by replacing the main character with a new one:\\n    - New character: Jose Rodriguez\\n    - Gender: male\\n    - Adjust pronouns accordingly.\\n    \\n    Story:\\n    (CNN) -- It has been nearly four weeks since Italians went to the polls and the country is still drifting rudderless without a functioning government. This week, political leaders a