      "text='NEW YORK (CNN) -- In the heart of Manhattan\\'s financial district, amidst the turmoil of a tumultuous Wall Street, a different sort of gathering was underway. The air was thick with the aroma of premium liquor and the lush clouds of cigar smoke. The mantra for the evening was a hedonistic one: Work hard, play hard. \\n\\n\"I was a scoundrel,\" admitted James Johnson, who once reigned as a high roller on Wall Street, raking in an incredible $1 million weekly before his fall from grace resulted in a stint behind bars.\\n\\nThomas Graf, vice president and producer of Northmarq Capital, shook his head in bemusement, \"It\\'s like playing the fiddle while Rome burned. We\\'re sitting here indulging in luxury while our financial world crumbles around us.\"\\n\\nThe party hosted by Cigar Report magazine was a rare haven for hundreds of mid-level executives and traders, an escape from the mounting pressures of the sliding stock market. The event, though perhaps tone-deaf to outside perceptions, offered a temporary sanctuary.\\n\\n\"This is indeed a welcome respite,\" said Adam Marsh, a member of the Empire Capital Partners. \"For a few precious hours, we can unwind, enjoy some fine spirits and ignore the impending storm on the Street tomorrow.\"\\n\\nMost attendees had enjoyed lucrative careers, reaping the rewards of the Wall Street bubble\\'s peak. Their success paled in comparison to the elite hedge fund managers and CEOs who reigned supreme.\\n\\nRobert Frank, author of \"Richistan,\" observed the extravagant lifestyles of this wealthy echelon. \"They lived in a world of opulence, where the metrics of success were measured by the size of their mansions, the exoticism of their cars, and the price tags on their artwork.\"\\n\\nOne such CEO, Richard Fuld of Lehman Brothers, earned an astonishing $45 million in salary and bonuses in a single year. The excesses didn\\'t go unscrutinized, as Fuld faced Congress to explain the collapse of his firm. \"The guilt I 