      "text='NEW YORK (CNN) -- He used to lunch at the exclusive Four Seasons. Now, the best-selling author jokes that he\\'s inviting friends to Taco Bell. Call it gallows humor, but James Johnson has just lost his life\\'s savings. Author and artist James Johnson says he lost his life savings investing with financier Bernard Madoff. He invested every penny with accused Ponzi-schemer Bernard Madoff. \\n\\nJohnson thought he had weathered the bear market just fine, since Madoff put his money in super-safe Treasuries. Then, on December 11, he received a call from his best friend. \"He said, \\'I hope this is a rumor, but I\\'ve just heard Bernie Madoff\\'s been arrested,\\' \" Johnson recalls. \"My other phone rang; it was my son. And he said, \\'Pop, sit down.\\' He said, \\'Bernie Madoff\\'s been arrested.\\' And I said, \\'For what?\\' And he said, \\'He\\'s a crook.\\' \" \\n\\nMadoff\\'s alleged $50 billion Ponzi scheme disrupted a fragile financial system, affecting hedge funds and well-heeled investors on Wall Street, Florida, and Europe. In a Ponzi scheme, money from new investors is used to pay off early investors to create the appearance of legitimate returns. Conservative with his money, Johnson says he has been working and saving since he was a teenager. He admits that his biggest fear was losing everything and \"becoming a bag man.\" \\n\\nAbout a decade ago, when Johnson was in his late 40s, he shared his fears with a good friend, who steered him to Madoff, where he thought his money would always be safe. Watch Johnson call Madoff \\'loathsome\\' » . Best known for the 1982 best-seller \"How to Make Love to a Man\" and as a former editor of Self Magazine, Johnson and his friend Evelyn Lauder were the first to use pink ribbons as a symbol for breast cancer awareness. Every success along the way meant more money, which Johnson eventually invested with Madoff. He earned so much that he was no longer writing and editing but living his dream as an artist. \\