ngers. But with increased American and Afghan troops on the ground, the situation is improving. \\n\\nShoutout: Who is the US Secretary of Health and Human Services? Answer: Kathleen Sebelius is responsible for protecting the health of Americans. \\n\\nH1N1 Response: Secretary Sebelius leads the US response to H1N1. The virus's spread has been classified as a pandemic by the World Health Organization. Experts explain how germs can spread in public spaces. For instance, if an infected person coughs and an individual behind them touches the same surface, they can contract the virus, which can survive on objects for 2 to 8 hours. \\n\\nNo More Fountains: To combat the spread of H1N1, some schools are disabling drinking fountains. Students are encouraged to bring their own water to school. This measure aims to reduce the transmission of the virus, which affects a large number of younger individuals. \\n\\nCollege Costs: Tuition fees continue to rise, making higher education more expensive. Public, in-state universities charge around $7,000 annually, while out-of-state public universities and private schools can cost over $18,000 and $26,000 per year, respectively. Room and board costs are additional. Financial aid assists two-thirds of full-time students, but others may resort to student loans. \\n\\nBefore we conclude, a story about a bowling tournament for seniors playing the Nintendo Wii. Around 600 older bowlers participated in the Texas event, setting a world record and promoting the importance of senior fitness. \\n\\nMiller: There you have it. Stay tuned for more student news coming up later. For CNN Student News, I'm David Miller.\" generation_id='d8c31379-a2d8-4da3-9916-9084958a7bc2' response_id='379ed018/e0d1a5d4-09ef-402f-891b-8fdebe94fbdf' citations=None documents=None is_search_required=None search_queries=None search_results=None finish_reason='COMPLETE' tool_calls=None chat_history=[UserMessage(role='USER', message='\\n    Rewrite the following story by r