      "text=\"James Johnson's mother, Patricia, has said that she blames herself for her son's suicide. \\n\\nJames had asked his mother to cut off all contact with his father, Richard, after his arrest for running the biggest financial fraud in history. But Patricia had remained steadfastly by Richard's side, causing a rift in their relationship.\\n\\nSpeaking for the first time since her husband's arrest, Patricia now wishes she had heeded her son's request. 'I just wish, until my dying day, that I had done what he wanted,' she said. 'I don't know if it would have made a difference, but at least I would have felt better.'\\n\\nThe suicide of her eldest son, who hanged himself with a dog leash, has torn Patricia apart. 'It's the most awful thing that can happen to anybody,' she said, her voice cracking with emotion.\\n\\nThe Johnson family had spoken infrequently in the two years following Richard's arrest, as his other children, Andrew and Sarah, struggled to comprehend their mother's unwavering loyalty to their father.\\n\\n'I never considered leaving,' Patricia explained. 'I didn't know what else to do besides stay.'\\n\\nAndrew Johnson found his brother's suicide particularly difficult to bear. 'He was my best friend,' he said, his eyes welling up with tears. 'It was terrible to see him struggling so desperately, and not be able to help him.'\\n\\nAfter his brother's tragic death, Andrew managed to persuade his mother to cut ties with Richard, and she now resides in a cozy three-room apartment in South Florida. The two reconciled, bringing some comfort to the difficult situation.\\n\\nWhen asked why she hadn't filed for divorce, Patricia's response was matter-of-fact. 'It doesn't matter—he's going to die in prison,' she said, referring to her husband's lengthy sentence.\\n\\nPatricia revealed that she and Richard had attempted suicide together on Christmas Eve, but had merely awoken from their drug-induced slumber the following day, apparently no worse off than before.\\n\\nHowever, Richard has since refuted this claim from behind bars, insisting that he never tried to take his own life.\\n\\nIn an emotional interview, Patricia looked at old photographs of her sons with their father, her face crumpling as she recalled happier times.\\n\\nThe interview was conducted ahead of the release of 'Truth and Consequences: Life Inside the Johnson Family,' a tell-all book written by author Laurie Sandell. The work delves into the family's painful experiences and includes never-before-seen snapshots of the Johnsons.\\n\\nSandell was approached by Andrew and his fiancée, Catherine Hooper, to pen the revealing account. It promises to shed light on the family's fall from grace and the aftermath of the financial scandal.\\n\\nIn the interview, Andrew spoke of his brother's final months, recalling his desperate attempts to understand and help Mark during his time of need. 'It was very painful for me,' Andrew said, his voice trembling. 'To see him struggling so desperately and not be able to help him was horrible.'\\n\\nAndrew and his sister have always maintained that they had no knowledge of their father's fraudulent activities. When Richard finally confessed, sobbing, Patricia's first question was, 'What's a Ponzi scheme?'\\n\\n'She didn't even understand the term,' Andrew explained. 'It was me who had to explain that it meant everything was fake.'\\n\\nRichard's investment advisory business, which operated as a Ponzi scheme, was carefully shielded by a legitimate trading business run by Andrew and James. 'We were completely busy executing countless transactions daily,' Andrew explained. 'The idea of my father's criminal activity never crossed my mind.'\\n\\nThe siblings had grown up in a life of luxury, wanting for nothing. Both had gone on to work for their father after graduating from college.\\n\\nAndrew admitted that one of the most difficult aspects of the scandal was the realization that he and his brother had been used by their 