the firm lost £4.86m, some 30 per cent of its capital. Peter Hunter, defending, said the con failed after the markets 'became turbulent' in late 2008. He said: 'Every time he did a trade and lost, instead of stopping he tried to double his trade to recoup the loss - he tried to trade his way out. Court: Judge Peter Thornton QC warned Oates that he faces deportation to his native South Africa, during the hearing at the Old Bailey today . 'Eventually, the losses became awful and he had no choice but to approach his employers. 'He has asked me to apologise to the court and to all concerned. He is thoroughly ashamed.' Oates first moved to the UK in 2004 after graduating with a degree in accounting and economics from Stellenbosch University, near Cape Town. Mr Hunter said: 'He came to this country, in fact, to seek his fortune. 'Whilst here, his mother came into difficulties with finances, and his brother became addicted to drugs, so he was under pressure to find money. 'The original desire was to help them. 'Every asset he had has been taken, and everything from his bank account to the house he lived in has been repossessed and taken back. 'There was no extravagant lifestyle, he wasn't driving a flash car. 'The profit to this man is nothing.' Oates admitted two counts of fraud by abuse of position.\\n    \\n    Provide only the rewritten story.\\n    \", tool_calls=None), ChatbotMessage(role='CHATBOT', message=\"James Johnson, a City trader who lost his employer £4.8m in a series of reckless gambles, has been handed a five-year sentence. \\n\\nJohnson, 31, of New Addington, Surrey, left TRX Futures with the huge financial loss after blowing the cash on risky deals. The firm took drastic measures to recover, including cutting salaries and laying off employees. Meanwhile, Johnson pocketed £775,000 from his fraudulent activities.\\n\\nThe Old Bailey heard how Johnson broke company rules by creating a fake trading account for his own benefit and forging records to show a false deposit. He also manipulated real clients' records, stealing their profits and using the money to purchase two houses. \\n\\nJudge Peter Thornton QC condemned Johnson's dishonest conduct, which lasted nearly two years, and noted that his actions harmed public confidence in the financial sector. The con artist had worked at TRX since 2006 and his fraud came to light when his employers discovered the massive losses. \\n\\nDespite the evidence, Johnson initially denied any wrongdoing to company managers and remained silent during the police interview. The court learned that Johnson, a South African national, came to the UK in 2004 and committed the fraud to help his struggling family. His defense argued that the fraud escalated as Johnson attempted to recover his losses by making more trades. \\n\\nJohnson pleaded guilty to two counts of fraud by abuse of position. The court ordered that all his assets be seized and Johnson faces deportation to his home country.\", tool_calls=None)] prompt=None meta=ApiMeta(api_version=None, billed_units=ApiMetaBilledUnits(images=None, input_tokens=776.0, output_tokens=312.0, search_units=None, classifications=None), tokens=ApiMetaTokens(input_tokens=776.0, output_tokens=312.0), warnings=None)\n",
