      "text='Jailed: James Johnson, 61, from Wrexham in Wales - pictured here with grey hair and sunglasses - tricked three women into investing their life savings through him. \\n\\nThe male Falklands RAF pilot callously duped his two elderly aunts and a third woman out of a total of £312,000 and was sentenced to two years in jail.\\n\\nBusinessman and financial advisor James Johnson, 61, from Wrexham in Wales, tricked the three women into investing their savings through him. One of his aunts, who suffers from dementia, was left £10,000 overdrawn and financially destroyed after Johnson squandered her money and that of the other two women on luxurious items.\\n\\nAt Caernarfon Crown Court in North Wales, Judge Niclas Parry condemned the despicable crimes, which had left three vulnerable, elderly victims financially devastated. \\'This case demonstrates a severe violation of the trust placed in the defendant by the victims,\\' the judge stated. \\'The victims trusted the defendant with their life savings, and he betrayed this trust in a deplorable manner.\\'\\n\\nJohnson admitted to seven fraud offences committed between 2002 and 2008, with £250,000 of the misappropriated funds remaining unaccounted for. The prosecutor, Jonathan Austin, stated that aunt Vera Davies, who is in her eighties, handed Johnson £35,000 to invest in an Australian company for a regular income, assured by him that her money was safe. However, Johnson did not invest the funds as promised. Instead, he paid Vera £27,000 in monthly installments, using her own money or that of others. In 2007, the payments ceased when Johnson encountered financial difficulties.\\n\\nDilys Williams, Vera\\'s sister who suffered from dementia, was defrauded out of £172,500. Being mentally impaired, she relied on Johnson\\'s assistance with her finances. In 2005, he was granted enduring power of attorney over her financial affairs, a responsibility he exploited. Johnson misappropriated her funds to support his struggling business interests. Using this power, he transferred money to himself. Unfortunately, Mrs. Williams\\' savings were depleted, leading to bounced cheques and eventually leaving her £10,000 in debt. The Court of Protection was forced to intervene and revoke his power of attorney.\\n\\nThe third victim, Judith Rich from Bath, aged 60, provided Johnson with four payments totaling £105,000, intending for him to invest the funds in Australia. In return, she expected a monthly income. Although she received £32,000 over the years, the payments ceased in 2007. When she requested the return of her money, Johnson failed to comply, forcing her to seek financial assistance from the local authority. The prosecutor noted that the misappropriated funds had been Judith\\'s \\'nest egg\\' for her future.\\n\\nThe defence attorney, John Wyn Williams, described the day of the trial as a \"tragic day\" for Johnson and his family, adding that they felt ashamed and embarrassed about his actions. According to the attorney, Johnson\\'s initial intention was to borrow the money temporarily and repay it later. Health problems and financial difficulties arising from a failed business venture in Pakistan were offered as explanations for his criminal behaviour.\\n\\nJohnson\\'s descent into criminality marked a stark contrast to his earlier accomplishments. He had been a head boy at school, excelled in sports, obtained a chemistry degree, and served as an RAF flight commander during the Falklands War. The court heard that there were numerous \\'glowing\\' testimonials about his character.\\n\\nThe judge showed little sympathy, emphasizing the severity of the crimes and the devastating impact on the victims. Johnson\\'s actions, he said, had led to the financial ruin of three innocent and vulnerable individuals.' generation_id='f7b3b34d-0dee-44f5-b8c4-791b34f08510' response_id='379ed018/9dd30581-7761-4ee0-a908-b535da4ec61b' citations=None documents=None is_search_required=None search_queries=