      "text=\"James Johnson made the counterfeit tickets and pocketed some money for the deception. It seemed like an easy way to travel first class without the hefty price tag. But Johnson and his two friends were caught out when an observant rail official noticed a forged ticket. \\n\\nThe three friends, all respected professionals, found themselves in hot water after it was discovered they had been involved in forging first class rail tickets and car park passes. The scam involved scanning old tickets into a computer and altering the dates. Johnson and his accomplices had successfully copied a first class monthly season ticket from Southampton to London Waterloo, worth £849.50, and a monthly railway car park ticket, costing £130.\\n\\nThe men, who had seemingly successful lives, were handed suspended jail sentences. The judge, Susan Evans QC, called them out for their arrogant and dishonest behaviour, causing significant financial loss to the railway company.\\n\\nIt started when a rail community officer noticed a suspicious fake parking ticket on the dashboard of one of the men's cars. Police investigations led them to Johnson's friend, who was found with more counterfeit tickets. Johnson, it was discovered, had created the forgeries and distributed them to his friends. \\n\\nJohnson's home was searched but no fake tickets were found; however, it was evident he was responsible for the scheme and had profited from it. A first class monthly rail ticket was discovered at one of his friend's homes.\\n\\nThe three men, all intelligent and gainfully employed, admitted to conspiring to commit fraud. Johnson was sentenced to 16 weeks in prison, suspended for two years, and ordered to complete 250 hours of unpaid work. His friends received lesser sentences, and the trio were collectively ordered to repay the defrauded amount of £2,219 and cover £1,500 in court costs.\" generation_id='f0c60d3f-783f-40fc-85bc-7680ce182b9e' response_id='379ed018/6f945018-90d4-45ea-94f3-6c8e