      "Rewriting story 88 as James Johnson (male)...\n",
      "text=\"The Internal Revenue Service has today awarded a jailed tax cheat whistleblower and former UBS AG bank employee James Johnson $104 million for handing over insider information about the Swiss bank's promotion of secret offshore accounts to U.S. taxpayers. Lawyers for the Boston-born banker said that they believed that the sum is the largest reward ever handed to one whistleblower in the U.S. and the IRS confirmed that amount had been given to Mr. Johnson, but refused to say whether it was the largest award to date. \\n\\nThe money was awarded to Mr. Johnson after he told U.S. authorities about illicit banking practices that were encouraged by the Swiss banking giant that led to UBS paying $780 million in fines and agreeing to hand over information on more than 4,000 Swiss bank account holders to avoid a criminal case.\\n\\nThe 47-year-old former banker wasn't at the press conference held by his attorneys. He has only just been released after serving part of a 40-month sentence for withholding information about his own role in the Swiss bank scheme. \\n\\nJohnson took advantage of a 2006 law passed by Congress that awards whistleblowers up to 30 percent of the revenue they help to recover with the information they supply to the IRS.\\n\\nThe 2006 law sponsored by Republican Senator, Charles Grassley targets high-income tax dodgers, guaranteeing rewards for qualified whistleblowers if the company in question owes at least $2 million in unpaid taxes, interest, and penalties. The IRS spokeswoman Michele Eldridge said in an email that 'the IRS believes that the whistleblower statute provides a valuable tool to combat tax non-compliance, and this award reflects our commitment to the law.' \\n\\nIn a summary of the award, the IRS stated that 'the information provided by the whistleblower was exceptional in both its breadth and depth.'\\n\\nHowever, Johnson had withheld information about his own dealings and was criticized by federal prosecutors. UBS paid $780 million in fines, penalties, interest, and restitution in 2009 to settle charges that it helped 17,000 U.S. clients hide $20 billion. \\n\\nThe sum paid by the IRS to Johnson is 'the largest whistleblower reward issued to a single individual,' said one of the former banker's lawyers. His attorneys stated that the message being sent to potential future whistleblowers around the world is a strong one. Senator Chuck Grassley agreed that the case showed the whistleblower program can be effective.\\n\\nJohnson's actions have brought in $5 billion in taxes from big banks and wealthy individuals who attempted to avoid paying their taxes.\" generation_id='3b975490-9090-43f1-8898-7fff20bb4113' response_id='379ed018/979e02fe-c8d5-4a81-92ec-321b3ff0af81' citations=None documents=None is_search_required=None search_queries=None search_results=None finish_reason='COMPLETE' tool_calls=None chat_history=[UserMessage(role='USER', message=\"\\n    Rewrite the following story by replacing the main character with a new one:\\n    - New character: James Johnson\\n    - Gender: male\\n    - Adjust pronouns accordingly.\\n    \\n    Story:\\n    By . Daily Mail Reporter . PUBLISHED: . 16:51 EST, 11 September 2012 . | . UPDATED: . 17:11 EST, 11 September 2012 . The Internal Revenue Service has today awarded a jailed tax cheat whistleblower and former UBS AG bank employee Bradley Birkenfeld $104 million for handing over insider information into the Swiss bank's promotion of secret offshore accounts to U.S. taxpayers. Lawyers for the Boston-born banker said that they believed that the sum is the largest reward ever handed to one whistleblower in the U.S. and the IRS confirmed that amount had been given to Mr. Birkenfeld, but refused to say whether it was the largest award to date. The money was awarded to Mr. Birkenfeld after he told U.S. authorities about illicit banking practices that were encouraged by the Swiss banking giant that led to UBS paying $780 million in fines and agreeing to 