ys later. The defendant has pleaded not guilty. His attorney, Mark Sacco, requested a dismissal, arguing that the matter could have been better handled outside the courtroom. Sacco also claimed that the monetary dispute amounted to around $40,000, which could easily be settled in a civil court. According to the prosecution, J.W. is owed at least $240,000 in wages and $79,000 in overtime, calculated based on the minimum wage. J.W. entered the country legally and worked for a family in New Jersey in 2005. After a relationship ended, leaving her homeless, she took up a live-in domestic servant role with the Georges in 2006 for $1,000 monthly. She didn't fill out any immigration paperwork and her visa status remained unchanged. 'In a bid to secure permanent legal status for herself and her family, she has turned against my client,' Sacco asserted. Assistant U.S. Attorney Richard Belliss stated that J.W. currently resides in the country on a temporary basis and is self-supporting. There's a possibility she might testify in the trial, along with the immigration officers who removed her from the defendant's residence. Officers claimed that J.W. was eventually ejected from the mansion through a side door and without her luggage, which was later returned to her, minus a diary. It is alleged that J.W. received approximately $26,000 for five years of cooking, cleaning, and childcare, working from 5:30 a.m. to almost midnight daily. With limited English skills, J.W. neither accessed healthcare services nor left the George residence frequently. She was also taken to an immigration lawyer but didn't receive any documentation. Prosecutors argue that if the defendant is convicted, the mansion could be claimed as a 'vehicle used in the commission of the crime'. Annie George's husband, Mathai Kolath George, a native Indian who built a successful hotel and real estate business in the U.S., died in a private plane crash in 2009, alongside his 11-year-old son and another individual. --s